\par
---\par
\par
### Guidelines for Level Selection and Relevance\par
\par
- **Use the last doji before the impulse move** for defining levels.\par
- For impulse moves down, use the **doji\rquote s low** as resistance after breakdown.\par
- For impulse moves up, use the **doji\rquote s high** as support after breakout.\par
- Consider **recency and number of touches** of a level:  \par
  - The **first touch is strongest**.\par
  - More recent touches maintain relevance.\par
- Discard levels that have been broken multiple times or are too old unless trading on very high timeframes.\par
- Levels often react to previous older levels; newer levels generally encapsulate older ones.\par
\par
---\par
\par
### Best Practices and Risk Management\par
\par
- Confirm levels with **volume spikes** or momentum surges before taking trades.\par
- Use **tight stop-losses** just beyond the level tested (e.g., below support when longing).\par
- Avoid entering prematurely without confirmation to reduce false breakouts.\par
- Recognize patterns prone to fakeouts, such as **double tops/bottoms**.\par
- For scalpers, draw levels on higher timeframes (hourly or daily) and trade on lower timeframes rather than drawing levels directly on low timeframes.\par
\par
---\par
\par
### Summary Table of Trading Level Types\par
\par
| Level Type               | Description                                     | Trade Action                        |\par
|--------------------------|------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|\par
| Support                  | Last form of support before an impulse move down | Long on retest or bounce           |\par
| Resistance               | Last form of resistance before an impulse move up | Short on retest or rejection       |\par
| First Test               | Initial test of a newly broken support/resistance | High probability trade setup       |\par
| Swing Failure Pattern    | Break and retrace of a level                     | Trade reversal (short or long)     |\par
| Pullback                 | Retracement after impulse move, retesting level | Enter continuation trade           |\par
| Piercing Reversal Pattern| Candle closes above 50% of previous large candle | Potential reversal entry           |\par
\par
---\par
\par
### Key Insights\par
\par
- **Impulse moves combined with doji candles** provide a clear and reliable framework for determining support and resistance.\par
- The **low or high of the last doji before an impulse move** is the precise level to watch.\par
- Confirmation via **volume and momentum** is critical to increase strike rate and reduce false signals.\par
- Trading using these levels allows for **earlier entries**, improving risk-reward ratios.\par
- The method is **applicable across multiple timeframes**, but levels should be drawn on higher timeframes for reliability.\par
- **First test trades** are some of the most profitable and should be actively sought.\par
- Avoid relying on **old, frequently broken levels**; focus on recent, well-tested levels instead.\par
\par
---\par
\par
This approach offers a **structured, repeatable process** that simplifies the identification and utilization of support and resistance levels, blending classic price action with volume-based confirmation, suitable for swing trading and scalping alike.\par
